WEBVTT

1
00:00:14.832 --> 00:00:19.795
One of my favorite tasks at Birtwick Park was carrying the mistress when the whole family rode together.

2
00:00:20.816 --> 00:00:25.659
The little girls would take turns on Merrylegs and Sir Oliver, while the squire usually rode Ginger.

3
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:31.723
I knew that the lady preferred me to Ginger because my mouth was more sensitive, making me easier

4
00:00:31.763 --> 00:00:32.864
and more pleasant to ride.

5
00:00:33.856 --> 00:00:35.157
That made Ginger envy me.

6
00:00:35.177 --> 00:00:41.681
"It's all because of the cruel men who trained me," she complained one day in the orchard.

7
00:00:42.842 --> 00:00:47.284
Wise old Sir Oliver heard her and said, "Don't be so hard on yourself.

8
00:00:48.625 --> 00:00:54.629
You carry the squire's weight easily and still maintain your gaits, your fine ways of walking.

9
00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:58.111
That's nothing to be ashamed of.

10
00:00:59.191 --> 00:01:03.945
Besides, we horses should be content and willing as long as we're treated kindly."

11
00:01:05.205 --> 00:01:06.746
He swished his tail for emphasis.

12
00:01:08.666 --> 00:01:12.407
Sir Oliver's tail was very short—only six or seven inches long.

13
00:01:13.528 --> 00:01:17.489
I'd often wondered what accident had shortened it, and so I asked him now.

14
00:01:17.509 --> 00:01:21.810
"Accident?" Sir Oliver let out a fierce snort.

15
00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:23.631
"It was no accident.

16
00:01:24.771 --> 00:01:32.121
When I was young, my original owner had it cut off on purpose—flesh, bone, and all!"

17
00:01:32.141 --> 00:01:33.021
"That's awful!"

18
00:01:33.381 --> 00:01:33.982
I exclaimed.

19
00:01:34.002 --> 00:01:37.563
"Indeed it was," Sir Oliver agreed.

20
00:01:37.583 --> 00:01:42.304
"And it was not only the pain, though that was bad enough.

21
00:01:43.805 --> 00:01:48.346
I am left without enough tail to brush the flies off my sides and hind legs.

22
00:01:49.586 --> 00:01:51.947
The rest of you can't imagine what that's like.

23
00:01:53.087 --> 00:01:57.950
They can bite me all day long, and I'm helpless to flick them away as you can without

24
00:01:57.990 --> 00:01:58.751
thinking about it."

25
00:02:00.272 --> 00:02:02.413
"But why did they cut off your tail?"

26
00:02:02.753 --> 00:02:03.474
Ginger asked.

27
00:02:03.494 --> 00:02:06.816
"For fashion," Sir Oliver replied.

28
00:02:07.877 --> 00:02:08.477
"Nothing more."

29
00:02:08.497 --> 00:02:14.901
"It must be fashion that makes some humans strap our heads up with the checkrein as well," Ginger

30
00:02:14.961 --> 00:02:17.023
said. "Yes.

31
00:02:17.563 --> 00:02:22.747
It's also fashion that makes them strap those blinkers to our heads when we drive so we can't

32
00:02:22.847 --> 00:02:25.905
see anything to the sides," Sir Oliver replied.

33
00:02:27.486 --> 00:02:29.107
Justice looked up from his grazing.

34
00:02:29.127 --> 00:02:36.472
"The blinkers are supposed to prevent horses from shying and causing accidents," he said in his calm way.

35
00:02:36.492 --> 00:02:39.934
"Then why don't they put them on riding horses?"

36
00:02:40.415 --> 00:02:40.855
I asked.

37
00:02:40.875 --> 00:02:45.958
"I suppose that's because of fashion," Justice admitted.

38
00:02:45.978 --> 00:02:54.204
"True. Blinkers are especially dangerous at night, when horses can see better than humans," Sir Oliver said.

39
00:02:55.880 --> 00:03:00.382
"I have heard of many accidents that could have been prevented if the horse had been able to

40
00:03:00.442 --> 00:03:01.403
see all around him."

41
00:03:02.583 --> 00:03:05.704
We horses were not the only ones who thought badly of such things.

42
00:03:06.785 --> 00:03:10.287
The squire might still put blinkers on us when we pulled a cart or carriage.

43
00:03:11.287 --> 00:03:13.608
But he disliked checkreins as much as Ginger did.

44
00:03:15.089 --> 00:03:19.070
One day out on the road, the squire and I passed a team of fine horses.

45
00:03:20.131 --> 00:03:22.052
The driver was a friend of the squire's.

46
00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:25.821
He asked the squire what he thought of the new team.

47
00:03:25.841 --> 00:03:29.962
"They're a handsome pair," the squire replied.

48
00:03:29.982 --> 00:03:33.683
"But I see you still insist on lessening their power."

49
00:03:35.863 --> 00:03:37.184
"Oh, you mean the checkrein?"

50
00:03:37.564 --> 00:03:38.444
the other man said.

51
00:03:38.464 --> 00:03:41.625
"I like to see my horses hold their heads up."

52
00:03:43.285 --> 00:03:47.246
"So do I, but I prefer to see them do so on their own," the squire said.

53
00:03:47.266 --> 00:03:53.508
"Besides, if you don't let them throw all their weight into their work, it wears them out faster."

54
00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.601
"Maybe you're right, my friend," the other man said.

55
00:03:57.621 --> 00:04:00.683
"I don't promise to change my ways.

56
00:04:01.463 --> 00:04:04.084
But I do promise to think about what you've said."

57
00:04:07.686 --> 00:04:11.828
The squire tried to discourage people from being cruel to animals in any way he could.

58
00:04:13.488 --> 00:04:15.969
For instance, one day we were riding toward home.

59
00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:21.532
We came into view of the Birtwick Park gate just in time to see a pony cart approaching.

60
00:04:22.572 --> 00:04:26.034
The delicate little pony was being driven by a large, strong man.

61
00:04:27.255 --> 00:04:30.056
When the cart neared the gate, the pony turned toward it.

62
00:04:31.757 --> 00:04:36.359
With a growl the man yanked the pony's head around so strongly that the pony almost fell over.

63
00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:39.921
But the pony recovered and started forward again.

64
00:04:41.482 --> 00:04:43.003
The man wasn't satisfied with that.

65
00:04:43.183 --> 00:04:45.944
He lifted his whip and began lashing the pony cruelly.

66
00:04:49.486 --> 00:04:52.348
The squire urged me forward until we were beside the cart.

67
00:04:53.568 --> 00:04:54.328
"Mr. Sawyer!"

68
00:04:54.708 --> 00:04:56.109
he called in a stern voice.

69
00:04:56.129 --> 00:04:59.150
"Is that pony made of flesh and blood?"

70
00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:03.671
"Yes, and temper too," the other man retorted.

71
00:05:03.691 --> 00:05:06.792
"I didn't ask him to turn there.

72
00:05:07.812 --> 00:05:10.493
He needs to learn to do only as I say."

73
00:05:10.513 --> 00:05:15.554
"You've driven that pony to my place many times," the squire said.

74
00:05:15.574 --> 00:05:18.835
"How could he know you weren't going there again?

75
00:05:19.955 --> 00:05:22.376
Remembering the turn only shows his intelligence.

76
00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:23.961
But that's not the point.

77
00:05:25.283 --> 00:05:28.306
Your cruel treatment of that creature shows your own poor character."

78
00:05:29.528 --> 00:05:30.809
Sawyer didn't respond to that.

79
00:05:32.271 --> 00:05:36.816
But he stopped whipping the pony and drove on, leaving me glad to have such a kind master.
